:db/id 17592186083137 :question/id #uuid "95e6de18-9036-55d2-a1cc-1be931c76d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9519"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d084334-b89a-5a33-9373-086018cdfbc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186083502 :question/id #uuid "ce6322f7-d266-5d91-8b01-0e2cdf67ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-124137"} {:db/id 17592186083503 :question/id #uuid "df047aef-8b90-502d-bc68-a6958f0969d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124138"} {:db/id 17592186259681 :question/id #uuid "6688982b-061e-548a-92f3-135ff8f3c6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124393"} {:db/id 17592186259682 :question/id #uuid "d53009d8-333f-5b2e-8589-e17e64e488ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-124405"} {:db/id 17592186259683 :question/id #uuid "dcd48373-682a-59a9-b5fa-04669fe0cfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-124409"} {:db/id 17592186259684 :question/id #uuid "bf7bd909-cf27-508b-8907-199d014908fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124410"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Factorial of Zero"} {:db/id 17592186083137 :question/id #uuid "95e6de18-9036-55d2-a1cc-1be931c76d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9519"} {:db/id 17592186083502 :question/id #uuid "ce6322f7-d266-5d91-8b01-0e2cdf67ddad" :question/math-academy-id "q-124137"} {:db/id 17592186083503 :question/id #uuid "df047aef-8b90-502d-bc68-a6958f0969d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-124138"} {:db/id 17592186259681 :question/id #uuid "6688982b-061e-548a-92f3-135ff8f3c6b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124393"} {:db/id 17592186259682 :question/id #uuid "d53009d8-333f-5b2e-8589-e17e64e488ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-124405"} {:db/id 17592186259683 :question/id #uuid "dcd48373-682a-59a9-b5fa-04669fe0cfbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-124409"} {:db/id 17592186259684 :question/id #uuid "bf7bd909-cf27-508b-8907-199d014908fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-124410"} {:db/id 17592186048543} {:db/id 17592186049243} {:db/id 17592186049358} {:db/id 17592186049433} {:db/id 17592186049793} {:db/id 17592186050268} {:activity/first-step {:db/id 17592186084109} :activity/id #uuid "12ac57b3-912d-50bb-9303-f36e976ef9a7" :activity/scope {:db/id 17592186049341 :topic/id #uuid "ed31886d-0791-5bac-b288-89fc1dc25d66" :topic/math-academy-id 774} :activity/steps [{:db/id 17592186084109 :step/content {:db/id 17592186084444 :tutorial/content "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4$! (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 1! &= 1 \\\\[5pt] 2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\[5pt] 3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\[5pt] 4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\\\[5pt] 5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1$:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9766f96-1407-57c0-ad92-13325cf88b1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3678 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a6be5cad-4dd4-5a68-ab1b-b8c5289b6481" :step/math-academy-id 20558 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084110}} {:db/id 17592186084110 :step/content {:db/id 17592186083217 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186083017 :question/id #uuid "9ad07c38-8ace-5900-818d-34ccacc8ee11" :question/math-academy-id "e-5667"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc445253-146a-5448-9bf3-d5b6c0d4c1f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186083789} {:db/id 17592186083926} {:db/id 17592186235557}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Factorials"} :step/id #uuid "37b01530-00da-565b-af9b-e815f4b7677c" :step/math-academy-id 20560 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084288}} {:db/id 17592186084111 :step/content {:db/id 17592186084445 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results.\n\nFor example, computing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10!}{8!}\n$$\n\nusing this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way!\n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} {\\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that\n\n$$\n10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8\n$$\n\nand then cancel the $8$! that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfc6a557-606a-5683-a8b9-e748e0e86d7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3679 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions that Contain Factorials"} :step/id #uuid "ae9e4e75-3dec-5850-8499-2d805a95c1b5" :step/math-academy-id 20561 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084112}} {:db/id 17592186084112 :step/content {:db/id 17592186083218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186083018 :question/id #uuid "22af250d-d219-5003-b972-aa912a0d6ee1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5668"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "203386bf-cdf1-5a78-92fd-36fababfb971" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186050843}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186083601} {:db/id 17592186083790} {:db/id 17592186234327} {:db/id 17592186256569} {:db/id 17592186287963}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Quotient of Factorials"} :step/id #uuid "3e9a8d12-132c-5636-b4d1-9938c908227e" :step/math-academy-id 20562} {:db/id 17592186084288 :step/content {:db/id 17592186083335 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186083137 :question/id #uuid "95e6de18-9036-55d2-a1cc-1be931c76d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9519"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d084334-b89a-5a33-9373-086018cdfbc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186083502} {:db/id 17592186083503} {:db/id 17592186259681} {:db/id 17592186259682} {:db/id 17592186259683} {:db/id 17592186259684}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Factorial of Zero"} :step/id #uuid "4a95a294-6283-51d5-afc1-2871270296ed" :step/math-academy-id 35635 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084111}}] :activity/title "Factorials" :activity/type {:db/id 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id 17592186083457} {:db/id 17592186084109 :step/content {:db/id 17592186084444 :tutorial/content "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4$! (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 1! &= 1 \\\\[5pt] 2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\[5pt] 3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\[5pt] 4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\\\[5pt] 5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1$:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9766f96-1407-57c0-ad92-13325cf88b1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3678 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a6be5cad-4dd4-5a68-ab1b-b8c5289b6481" :step/math-academy-id 20558 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084110}} {:db/id 17592186084444 :tutorial/content "The **factorial** of a natural number is the product of the number and all of the natural numbers below it. The factorial of a number is denoted by placing an exclamation point after the number.\n\nFor example, the factorial of $4$ is written as $4$! (with an exclamation point after the $4$), and is computed as\n\n$$\n4! = 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24\n$$\n\nSo, we say that \"four factorial is twenty-four\".\n\nThe factorials of the natural numbers $1$ through $5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 1! &= 1 \\\\[5pt] 2! &= 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 \\\\[5pt] 3! &= 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 6 \\\\[5pt] 4! &= 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 24 \\\\[5pt] 5! &= 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1 = 120 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the factorial of $0$ is defined to be equal to $1$:\n\n$$\n0! = 1\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9766f96-1407-57c0-ad92-13325cf88b1a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3678 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id 17592186084110 :step/content {:db/id 17592186083217 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186083017 :question/id #uuid "9ad07c38-8ace-5900-818d-34ccacc8ee11" :question/math-academy-id "e-5667"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc445253-146a-5448-9bf3-d5b6c0d4c1f0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186083789} {:db/id 17592186083926} {:db/id 17592186235557}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Factorials"} :step/id #uuid "37b01530-00da-565b-af9b-e815f4b7677c" :step/math-academy-id 20560 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084288}} {:db/id 17592186084288 :step/content {:db/id 17592186083335 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186083137 :question/id #uuid "95e6de18-9036-55d2-a1cc-1be931c76d3f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9519"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d084334-b89a-5a33-9373-086018cdfbc3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186083502} {:db/id 17592186083503} {:db/id 17592186259681} {:db/id 17592186259682} {:db/id 17592186259683} {:db/id 17592186259684}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Factorial of Zero"} :step/id #uuid "4a95a294-6283-51d5-afc1-2871270296ed" :step/math-academy-id 35635 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084111}} {:db/id 17592186084111 :step/content {:db/id 17592186084445 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results.\n\nFor example, computing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10!}{8!}\n$$\n\nusing this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way!\n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} {\\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that\n\n$$\n10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8\n$$\n\nand then cancel the $8$! that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cfc6a557-606a-5683-a8b9-e748e0e86d7c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3679 :tutorial/title "Simplifying Rational Expressions that Contain Factorials"} :step/id #uuid "ae9e4e75-3dec-5850-8499-2d805a95c1b5" :step/math-academy-id 20561 :step/next {:db/id 17592186084112}} {:db/id 17592186084445 :tutorial/content "One way to simplify a rational expression containing factorials is to calculate the numerator and denominator separately, and then divide the results.\n\nFor example, computing\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10!}{8!}\n$$\n\nusing this method, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} {8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 1} = \\dfrac {3\\,628\\,800} {40\\,320} = 90. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the method above involves lots of computations. Thankfully, there's an easier way!\n\nBefore we multiply, we can cancel out any numbers that appear in both the numerator and denominator, thereby reducing the amount of computation that's needed. Using this method, we get the same result with far fewer computations:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} &= \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} {\\cancel{8} \\cdot \\cancel{7} \\cdot \\cancel{6} \\cdot \\cancel{5} \\cdot \\cancel{4} \\cdot \\cancel{3} \\cdot \\cancel{2} \\cdot \\cancel{1}} = \\dfrac{10 \\cdot 9}{1} = 90 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEven quicker, we could just realize that\n\n$$\n10! = 10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8\n$$\n\nand then cancel the $8$! that appears in both the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\dfrac {10!}{8!} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot \\cancel{8!}} {\\cancel{8!}} = \\dfrac {10 \\cdot 9} {1} = 90\n$$" :tutoria